<Base
  title="EKHO Labs — AI is only as good as your context"
  description="EKHO turns what you know into connected context that compounds, so you get more out of AI every time you use it, and it stays yours."
>
  <h1>AI is only as good as your context.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Make yours compound, and keep it. EKHO turns what you know into one connected source your AI can
    work with, so every time you use it, you get more out of it than the time before.
  </p>

  <h2>What context is</h2>
  <p>
    Context is everything an AI would need to know to answer the way you would, if you had the time
    to explain. Your situation, your goals, what you have already decided and why, what you have
    read and what you concluded from it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model brings intelligence. It is the same for everyone. You bring context. It exists only
    with you.
  </p>
  <p>The context window is where context gets used. EKHO is where it lives.</p>

  <h2>Why context matters</h2>
  <ol class="cards">
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Everyone has the same models. Nobody has your context.</h3>
      <p>
        As intelligence gets cheap, context is what separates a generic answer from one that
        actually helps you. It is the part of AI you control.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Most of it is implicit, or scattered.</h3>
      <p>
        In your head, in chats that close, in mails, meetings and notes that know nothing of each
        other. So every session starts near zero.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Connected, context compounds.</h3>
      <p>
        EKHO turns what was scattered into one highly connected source: every insight knows where it
        came from, what supports it and what contradicts it. Each new piece links to everything
        already there.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>An asset needs an owner.</h3>
      <p>
        If your context decides how much AI does for you, it has to stay yours. Not locked in a
        model provider's memory.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>AI does the bookkeeping.</strong> You bring in what comes up: a meeting, an article, a
    voice note. An agent condenses it, links it to what you already have, and marks what confirms or
    contradicts what. Your knowledge is sorted before you ask, not searched for when you do. Keeping
    it up costs almost nothing, which is why it compounds instead of gathering dust.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A standard that is enforced, not just written down.</strong> Your context lives in an
    open, typed format, and a linter keeps the agent in line whichever model is running. The model
    is replaceable. Your context stays with you.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>
    A folder of plain Markdown files that belongs to you and that you can take anywhere. The
    <code>ekho</code> command-line tool maintains it with the coding agent of your choice, such as
    Claude Code, Codex or OpenCode. You read the files, your AI reads the same ones.
  </p>
  <p>Throw it in, let it be digested, ask.</p>

  <h2>What does not exist yet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No web interface. You work in your editor and your terminal, and read in Obsidian.</li>
    <li>No automatic import. Mail, chat and calendar are not connected; you bring things in.</li>
    <li>No built-in sharing between people yet.</li>
    <li>EKHO takes over the bookkeeping, not your judgement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://get.ekholabs.eu/i/&lt;key&gt; | sh</code></pre>
  <p class="note">EKHO is in early access, by invitation. This installer is not available yet.</p>
</Base>
